[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Visual Comparisons and Snapshot Testing in Playwright"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Visual comparisons and snapshot testing allow you to catch unintended UI changes by comparing screenshots of your web application against baseline images. Playwright provides built-in capabilities for visual regression testing using the toHaveScreenshot() method."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How Snapshot Testing Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you run a visual comparison test for the first time, Playwright takes a screenshot and saves it as the baseline. On subsequent test runs, Playwright takes a new screenshot and compares it pixel by pixel against the baseline image. If the images differ beyond the allowed threshold, the test fails."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing a Basic Visual Comparison Test"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is an example of how to write a simple screenshot comparison test using Playwright."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('homepage visual comparison', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://example.com');
  await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('homepage.png');
});"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Updating Baselines"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"When your UI intentionally changes, you need to update your baseline snapshots. You can do this by running Playwright with the update-snapshots flag from your command line."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"npx playwright test --update-snapshots"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Tolerances and Options"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Different operating systems render fonts and graphics slightly differently. To account for minor rendering discrepancies, you can configure thresholds and options within the toHaveScreenshot method."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('dashboard.png', {
  maxDiffPixelRatio: 0.05,
  threshold: 0.2,
});"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Visual Testing"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hide dynamic elements such as timestamps, animated carousels, or user avatars before taking snapshots."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Run visual tests inside a consistent environment like Docker to avoid OS-specific font rendering issues."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Target specific locators instead of full pages when testing isolated components to reduce flaky test results."}]
